1. Introduction

This software is an improved version of KLIC (Ver 2.002) with
optimized compiler and runtime system for efficient goal scheduling.

2. Feature of the Software

Since the original version of KLIC dynamically schedules the execution
order of goals, its scheduling overhead may cause significant
performance degradation if your program consists of, for example,
communicating sequential processes.  This optimized version aims to
reduce the scheduling overhead by compile-time dependency analysis and
run-time scheduling exploiting the analysis result.

The compiler statically analyzes the dependency among goals to find
threads.  Since a thread consists of goals whose order of execution is
statically determined, the runtime system efficiently executes the
thread without scheduling overhead.  Moreover, this new scheduling
mechanism will drastically reduces GC invocation because it maintains
the execution environment of a thread using a stack.

This version also has new features; optimization of inner-thread goal
scheduling, and reply-first scheduling.

If a goal p/n is always followed by a goal q/m, the code of the clause
p/n is optimized to directly jump to the code of clause q/m.

In the parallel execution, reply-first scheduling is available.
In this scheduling, data requests from other nodes promote the scheduling
of the concerning threads.  This scheduling improve the response time
to the data request and reduces the idling time of nodes.


Note that the current version of this software does NOT support the all
features of the original KLIC.

3. Usage

The following instructions are the differences from the original KLIC.
See the documents of KLIC for common instructions not shown here.

3.1  Installation

Do "Configure" in the top directory, and then do "make."

Follow the following special instructions on "Configure."

  + Optimization level of C compiler should be "none", that is;

    Which optimization level to use? [none] none
                                            ~~~~

3.2  Compilation

The installation generates two compilers; the original version kl1cmp
and the optimizing version kl1cmp-p.

The compiler-driver klic has the following new options :

  + -e Optimize goal scheduling.

  + -E Optimize goal scheduling and make reply-first scheduling.

Specify kl1cmp-p as the KL1 compiler when these options are specified.

In directories sched-test/, Makefile and test programs are provided.
"make" in sched-test/ generates the following objects for each sample
source xxx.kl1 :

  +xxx          Compiled by original KLIC.
  +xxx-sched    Goal scheduling is optimized.
  +xxx-reply    Goal scheduling is optimized and also make
                reply-first scheduling.


3.3  Execution

The following options are available:

  + -S1 <n> : stack size for each thread (default = 100 word)
  + -S2 <n> : number of stacks for threads (default = 10)

Default values can be changed by modifying the values of STACKSIZE and
STACKNUM in the file "include/klic/sched.h".


4. Restrictions

This version has the following restrictions.

4.1  Restrictions on Programming

  + The program must be well-moded.
  + The @priority pragma cannot be used.
  + Only the following basic built-in predicates are implemented.
      (a) =/2, wait/1, current_priority/1, current_node/2
      (b) atomic/1
      (c) atom/1, make_atom/2, atom_number/2, get_atom_string/2,
          intern/2, get_atom_name/2
      (d) integer/1, :=/2
      (e) arithmetics such as add/3 , and macros such as '+' and '-'.
      (f) comparisons such as equal/2, and macros such as '=:='.
      (g) functor/3, new_functor/3, arg/3, setarg/4
      (h) list/1
      (i) io:outstream/1

4.2  Restrictions on Compilation and Execution

  + Separated compilation is not available.
  + Runtime tracing is not available.
